From: Luke Johnson <jaewun@me.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: keep vblank_refcount in sync on ppdone timeout

When a command-mode encoder misses the PP TX-DONE interrupt,
_dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_handle_ppdone_timeout() unregisters the
RDPTR/TE callback so a dead link cannot keep interrupting:

	msm_disp_snapshot_state(drm_enc->dev);
	dpu_core_irq_unregister_callback(phys_enc->dpu_kms,
			phys_enc->irq[INTR_IDX_RDPTR]);

That unregister bypasses the refcount that
dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_control_vblank_irq() owns, and the refcount is
what decides whether a later registration happens at all -- it only
calls dpu_core_irq_register_callback() on a 0 -> 1 transition:

	if (enable) {
		if (phys_enc->vblank_refcount == 0)
			ret = dpu_core_irq_register_callback(...);

So after the first ppdone timeout the encoder is left claiming a
vblank reference it no longer holds.  The next disable decrements the
stale count instead of reaching zero naturally, which means the
following enable sees 1 -> 2 and skips registration again.  The
RDPTR/TE interrupt stays off, and in the meantime wait_for_vblank()
can only time out.

dpu_encoder_helper_hw_reset() does not undo it either: it resets the
CTL block and clears enable_state, but never re-arms the vblank
callback.  Upstream's refcount fix (drm/msm/dpu: Modify vblank_refcount
if error in callback) only covers a failing register/unregister, not
this force-unregister, so the desync is still reachable.

Take vblank_ctl_lock and drive the count to zero in the same place the
callback is dropped, so the enable-side path can register again.  Drive it
down only when dpu_core_irq_unregister_callback() reports success: it
returns int, and zeroing the count while a failed unregister left the
callback installed would desynchronise the count from the callback.

Observed on AYN Thor (SM8550, DSI-2 1080x1920@120Hz, enc38) as an
intermittent top-panel stall across the idle -> active edge of the
link: "dpu_encoder_frame_done_timeout" / "kickoff timeout ... cnt 1" /
"failed wait_for_idle ret:-110".  This patch does not attempt to fix
the stall itself, only the interrupt accounting that leaves the
encoder degraded after it.

Tested by inspection and by 'git apply --check' against the synced
ROCKNIX SM8550 tree.  No AYN Thor hardware was available to exercise
the stall, so the behavioural claim above comes from the device
journal signature, not from a before/after run.

---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_cmd.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_cmd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_encoder_phys_cmd.c
@@ -163,6 +163,27 @@
 	phys_enc->irq[INTR_IDX_UNDERRUN] = phys_enc->hw_intf->cap->intr_underrun;
 }
 
+static void _dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_drop_te_irq(
+		struct dpu_encoder_phys *phys_enc)
+{
+	mutex_lock(&phys_enc->vblank_ctl_lock);
+	if (phys_enc->vblank_refcount > 0 &&
+			!dpu_core_irq_unregister_callback(phys_enc->dpu_kms,
+				phys_enc->irq[INTR_IDX_RDPTR])) {
+		/*
+		 * Only a successful unregister means the callback is really
+		 * gone. It is the callback that owns the reference
+		 * control_vblank_irq() counts, so clear the count only when
+		 * the unregister dropped it: leaving a stale count makes the
+		 * next enable-side registration skip, but zeroing it while
+		 * the callback is still installed would desynchronise the
+		 * two.
+		 */
+		phys_enc->vblank_refcount = 0;
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&phys_enc->vblank_ctl_lock);
+}
+
 static int _dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_handle_ppdone_timeout(
 		struct dpu_encoder_phys *phys_enc)
 {
@@ -200,8 +219,7 @@
 			  cmd_enc->pp_timeout_report_cnt,
 			  atomic_read(&phys_enc->pending_kickoff_cnt));
 		msm_disp_snapshot_state(drm_enc->dev);
-		dpu_core_irq_unregister_callback(phys_enc->dpu_kms,
-				phys_enc->irq[INTR_IDX_RDPTR]);
+		_dpu_encoder_phys_cmd_drop_te_irq(phys_enc);
 	}
 
 	atomic_add_unless(&phys_enc->pending_kickoff_cnt, -1, 0);
